        heading: 'Avoiding One-and-Done Thinking',
        body: `The most common mistake small-business owners make with acquisition spend is measuring an ad campaign, a referral program, or a piece of content purely against the revenue from the very next sale it produces. This is one-and-done thinking, and it leads to two specific errors.

The first error is killing a channel too early. A campaign that loses money on the first purchase but attracts customers who stay for years can be one of the most valuable channels a business has, but it will look like a failure if judged only on day-one numbers.

The second error is overspending on customers who will not return. A channel that produces cheap first-time sales but attracts one-time bargain shoppers with no repeat behavior can look efficient on paper while quietly costing the business money once fulfillment and service costs are included.

The fix for both errors is the same: track, even roughly, whether the customers a channel brings in actually behave like the "average customer" used in the LTV formula, and adjust the formula and the acquisition ceiling for that channel accordingly.`,
