        heading: 'Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC)',
        body: `CAC is the fully loaded cost to acquire one new customer, calculated as:

CAC = Total sales and marketing spend over a period ÷ Number of new customers acquired in that period

"Fully loaded" matters: CAC should include ad spend, but also the cost of the tools running the campaign, the commission or hourly cost of a salesperson closing the deal, and any agency or contractor fees tied to acquisition — not just the raw media spend. A business that only counts ad spend and ignores sales labor will systematically underestimate its true acquisition cost and may scale spend based on a number that is too optimistic.

CAC should also be tracked separately by channel wherever possible (referral, paid search, paid social, organic, etc.), because a blended average CAC can hide the fact that one channel is highly efficient and another is quietly losing money, with the average masking both.`,
