        body: `Contribution margin is the revenue from a customer or sale minus the variable costs directly tied to delivering it (materials, direct labor, payment processing fees, shipping, and similar costs that scale with volume) — excluding fixed overhead like rent or salaried staff who would be paid regardless of any single sale.

Contribution margin = Revenue per unit − Variable cost per unit

Contribution margin, not gross revenue, is the correct figure to use when deciding how much you can afford to spend on acquisition, because it reflects what is actually left over from a sale to pay for acquisition costs and fixed overhead. A business that calculates its acquisition budget from revenue instead of contribution margin will systematically overspend, since it has not accounted for the direct cost of delivering the product or service in the first place.

For a recurring or subscription-style business, contribution margin is usually expressed per month per customer; for a project-based or one-time-purchase business, it is usually expressed per project or per transaction.`,
