        body: `Framing effects, also grounded in Kahneman and Tversky's research (notably their 1981 paper on the "Asian disease problem," which showed that identical outcomes described as "lives saved" versus "lives lost" produced very different decisions from the same people), describe how the same factual information leads to different choices depending on how it is worded.

For marketing, common honest framing choices include: describing price as a small recurring amount ("$3 a day") rather than a large annual figure when that framing genuinely helps a customer understand affordability, or leading with a specific outcome ("90% of roofs we inspect need only minor repair, not replacement") rather than a generic feature list, when the statistic is accurate and representative.

Framing becomes manipulative when it is used to obscure a fact the customer would want to know in making a rational decision — for example, advertising "$29 a month" prominently while hiding a mandatory $400 annual fee in fine print, or framing a subscription's cancellation terms in confusing language specifically so customers do not realize they are locked in. The test is not whether framing was used (framing is unavoidable — every sentence frames something) but whether the frame helps or actively obstructs the customer's understanding of the real deal.`,
